  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Dreier studied at Yale and Harvard Law School before practising at established New York firms. He later founded Dreier LLP, pursuing an unusual structure without conventional partners and expanding it to roughly 250 lawyers across several offices. The firm gave him professional stature, but its growth and his spending increasingly depended on money raised outside legitimate legal work.\n\nDreier fabricated promissory notes, financial statements and audit materials, then used impersonators and false meetings to persuade investment funds that the instruments were genuine. The scheme raised hundreds of millions of dollars before collapsing in 2008. He pleaded guilty, received a twenty-year federal sentence and was disbarred, turning a prominent legal career into a defining case of professional fraud.",
